 #edb/ref 17592186498411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498524 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143080" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498524 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498524 #edb/ref 1071 "The length, in centimeters, of newborn children in a particular hospital is distributed with a population standard deviation of $\\sigma =5\\,cm.$ On a particular day in a nursery room of the hospital, there are $64$ newborn children, and their mean length is $49.5\\,cm.$ Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean length $\\mu $ of the children born in that hospital.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498524 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40cf6769-0da8-5620-85fa-c881e7916a83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498524 #edb/ref 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=64\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=49.5\\,cm,\\,n=64,\\,\\sigma =5\\,cm.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ac93fdb7b03a2bf9cad38c9e5687867a2fa2968120586f3bf2e6101c24cbe.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49.5\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{64}} \\\\ 49.5\\pm 1.61\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498542 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498542 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498542 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=135$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =1.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-2,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498542 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64f21f0a-233c-5960-8d23-c7d29d8f3ed3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498542 #edb/ref 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=135\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.10\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{135}} \\\\ -2\\pm 0.142\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498557 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498557 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143022" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498557 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498557 #edb/ref 1071 "In order to measure the acceleration due to gravity, Annie knocks a ball off a table and records the time it takes to reach the ground. The measurements of the falling time of the ball are normally distributed with standard deviation $\\sigma =0.2\\,s.$ She repeats the experiment $16$ times and obtains a mean falling time of $0.55\\,s.$ Find a $90%$ confidence interval for the mean falling time $\\mu .$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498557 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "841adb19-e83e-5b6e-be60-db36b3558aba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498557 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=0.55\\,s,\\,n=16,\\,\\sigma =0.2\\,s\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/2f/2fc6327df99c6b88508b0c4de2e4edf6c5521974f05e2e2f02d09786937285a7.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.05$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.55\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\ 0.55\\pm 0.08\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498580 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498580 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142503" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498580 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498580 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=225$  from a population with s